# Check that the user is not logged in #

<who>

# Change the username #

<sudo usermod -l newuser olduser>

Rename the home directory

#sudo mv /home/olduser /home/newuser don't use

if used use the this command: sudo usermod -d /home/newuser newuser
or sudo chown -R newuser:newuser /home/newuser


Update the home directory path for the user

sudo usermod -d /home/newuser -m newuser

Rename the user's group (if it exists)

getent group olduser

sudo groupmod -n newuser olduser

Check Ownership of Files and Directories

sudo find / -user old_username -exec chown -h new_username {} \;

Check for Files Owned by user using UID:

sudo find / -uid old_user_UID -exec chown -h ab-admin {} \;

To recreate the user with same uid

sudo useradd -u 1001 dw

To change the ownership to new user

sudo find / -user old_username -exec chown -h new_username {} \;

Skip Protected System Directories:
You can exclude system directories like /proc, /sys, and /dev that may cause this error:

sudo find / -uid 1001 \( -path /proc -o -path /sys -o -path /dev \) -prune -o -exec sudo chown -h ab-admin {} \;

Identify the Primary Group of the User.

id new_username


Locate Shell Profile Files

Check the .bashrc or .profile file for any reference to the old path /home/old_user. Run:

grep -r "/home/AB-DEV-11" ~/.bashrc ~/.profile

nano ~/.bashrc

Look for lines like:

source /home/AB-DEV-11/Documents/git-prompt.sh

Update Global Configurations (If Necessary)

If this path is referenced in global configuration files, search /etc for references to /home/old_user:

sudo grep -r "/home/old_user" /etc

Replace References with sed
You can use the sed command to replace all instances of /home/AB-DEV-11 with /home/ab-dev-11 across multiple files. Here’s how:

sudo find /home/ab-dev-11 -type f -exec sed -i 's|/home/AB-DEV-11|/home/ab-dev-11|g' {} +

Update sudoers (if manually set)

sudo visudo
sudo ls /etc/sudoers.d/
sudo nano /etc/sudoers.d/olduser

View /etc/passwd File: This file stores user account information. Each line represents a user.

cat /etc/passwd


You'll see lines like:

username:x:UID:GID:User Info:Home Directory:Shell

List Only Usernames: If you want just the usernames, you can filter the output:

cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd

Use getent Command: This also displays users from /etc/passwd.

getent passwd
